Subject: corelib: no timezone tables, no signals, no flock on wasi

WHAT / WHY

  src/corelib/global/qtenvironmentvariables.cpp
      qTzSet() calls tzset() and qTzName() reads tzname[]. wasi-libc keeps
      tzset, tzname[], daylight and timezone behind
      __wasilibc_unmodified_upstream with the comment "WASI has no timezone
      tables", so both are COMPILE errors ("use of undeclared identifier").
      qTzSet() becomes a no-op -- a component's clock is UTC and there is no TZ
      database to re-read -- and qTzName() reports the empty string, which is
      what callers already treat as "unknown zone, use the offset".

      Note this file is compiled unconditionally; FEATURE_timezone=OFF does not
      reach it.

  src/corelib/io/qlockfile_unix.cpp
      QLockFilePrivate::isProcessRunning() calls ::kill(pid, 0) to decide
      whether a lock file is stale. wasi-libc declares kill() only under
      __wasilibc_unmodified_upstream ("WASI has no signals") and defines no
      symbol for it, so it fails at compile time.

      Returns false (== "not running", so the lock is stale and gets broken).
      That is the only answer that lets a node restart after a crash instead of
      deadlocking on its own leftover lock, and a wasip2 component genuinely
      cannot observe another process.

      The cost is real and worth writing down: if two wk nodes ever share one
      writable volume, each will consider the other's lock stale. QLockFile is
      NOT a cross-node mutex on this platform.

      A SECOND hunk in the same file `#undef`s LOCK_EX and LOCK_NB, reusing the
      idiom upstream already has three lines above for Q_OS_RTEMS. wasi-libc's
      <sys/file.h> declares flock() and defines LOCK_SH/LOCK_EX/LOCK_NB/LOCK_UN
      while no wasi-sdk library defines the symbol, so setNativeLocks()'s
      `#elif defined(LOCK_EX) && defined(LOCK_NB)` branch compiles happily and
      the failure lands at APPLICATION link time as "undefined symbol: flock",
      long after anyone is still thinking about QLockFile. Undefining the
      macros selects the existing `#else Q_UNUSED(fd)` no-op branch.

UPSTREAM
  yes for the qtenvironmentvariables.cpp hunks -- a libc that has no TZ tables
  is an ordinary porting case and the guards sit next to the existing
  Q_OS_WIN/_UCRT ones.

  not-as-is for qlockfile_unix.cpp. The guard is fine but the SEMANTICS are a
  policy choice (always-stale) that upstream would want to argue about, and it
  silently weakens QLockFile. If wk ever needs a real cross-node lock this hunk
  is the first thing to revisit.

---
diff --git a/src/corelib/global/qtenvironmentvariables.cpp b/src/corelib/global/qtenvironmentvariables.cpp
index 7bc717e..7151390 100644
--- a/src/corelib/global/qtenvironmentvariables.cpp
+++ b/src/corelib/global/qtenvironmentvariables.cpp
@@ -345,6 +345,12 @@ void qTzSet()
     const auto locker = qt_scoped_lock(environmentMutex);
 #if defined(Q_OS_WIN)
     _tzset();
+#elif defined(Q_OS_WASI)
+    // wasi-libc keeps tzset(), tzname[], daylight and timezone behind
+    // __wasilibc_unmodified_upstream with the comment "WASI has no timezone
+    // tables", so this is a compile error rather than a link error. A wasip2
+    // component's clock is UTC and there is no TZ database to re-read, so
+    // there is genuinely nothing to do here.
 #else
     tzset();
 #endif // Q_OS_WIN
@@ -417,6 +423,13 @@ QString qTzName(int dstIndex)
         const auto locker = qt_scoped_lock(environmentMutex);
         ok = _get_tzname(&s, name, 512, dstIndex) != 0;
     }
+#elif defined(Q_OS_WASI)
+    // No tzname[] on wasi-libc -- see qTzSet() above. Reporting "no name" is
+    // the honest answer for a component whose only zone is UTC; callers treat
+    // an empty QString as "unknown zone" and fall back to the offset.
+    Q_UNUSED(dstIndex);
+    Q_UNUSED(name);
+    ok = false;
 #else
     {
         const auto locker = qt_scoped_lock(environmentMutex);
diff --git a/src/corelib/io/qlockfile_unix.cpp b/src/corelib/io/qlockfile_unix.cpp
index 5704f65..aa1ce78 100644
--- a/src/corelib/io/qlockfile_unix.cpp
+++ b/src/corelib/io/qlockfile_unix.cpp
@@ -30,6 +30,19 @@
 #  undef LOCK_NB
 #endif
 
+#if defined(Q_OS_WASI)
+// Same treatment as RTEMS above, but the failure mode is nastier: wasi-libc's
+// <sys/file.h> DECLARES flock() and defines LOCK_SH/LOCK_EX/LOCK_NB/LOCK_UN,
+// while no wasi-sdk library defines the symbol (verified with llvm-nm over
+// libc.a, librt.a and every libwasi-emulated-*). So the guard below is
+// satisfied, QtCore compiles clean, and the failure lands much later as
+// "undefined symbol: flock" when an APPLICATION links -- long after anyone is
+// still looking at QLockFile. Undefining the macros routes setNativeLocks()
+// to its existing no-op branch.
+#  undef LOCK_EX
+#  undef LOCK_NB
+#endif
+
 #include <sys/types.h> // kill
 #include <signal.h>    // kill
 #include <unistd.h>    // gethostname
@@ -185,6 +198,25 @@ bool QLockFilePrivate::removeStaleLock()
 
 bool QLockFilePrivate::isProcessRunning(qint64 pid, const QString &appname)
 {
+#ifdef Q_OS_WASI
+    // wasi-libc declares kill() only under __wasilibc_unmodified_upstream
+    // ("WASI has no signals"), and there is no symbol for it in libc.a either,
+    // so this is a compile error before it would have been a link error.
+    //
+    // There is also no meaningful answer to give. A wasip2 component is alone
+    // in its world: it cannot enumerate, signal or even name another process.
+    // Reporting "not running" means a lock file left behind by an earlier run
+    // is treated as stale and broken, which is the behaviour that lets a node
+    // restart after a crash instead of deadlocking on its own leftovers.
+    //
+    // The cost, stated plainly: if two wk nodes ever share one writable volume,
+    // each will consider the other's lock stale. QLockFile is therefore NOT a
+    // cross-node mutex on this platform. Guarding a shared volume needs
+    // something built on wk's own capabilities, not on POSIX signals.
+    Q_UNUSED(pid);
+    Q_UNUSED(appname);
+    return false;
+#else
     if (::kill(pid_t(pid), 0) == -1 && errno == ESRCH)
         return false; // PID doesn't exist anymore
 
@@ -198,6 +230,7 @@ bool QLockFilePrivate::isProcessRunning(qint64 pid, const QString &appname)
     }
 
     return true;
+#endif // Q_OS_WASI
 }
 
 QString QLockFilePrivate::processNameByPid(qint64 pid)
